          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 18,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "k"
        }
      ],
      "why": "unrolled_k chains k micro-rotations per cycle, so it needs only Fmax >= 50*(ceil(N/k)+3) MHz. With k up to 8 and N 12..16, the required Fmax drops to roughly 350..550 MHz, which is much more realistic for sky130 sc_hd than the iterative case. This makes unrolled_k the family most likely to actually satisfy the 50 MSPS throughput constraint while keeping power low (still a shared datapath, just wider combinational logic). I keep the same accuracy-relevant ranges as iterative so the two fronts are directly comparable, and let k span 2..8 so the search can trade off the required Fmax against the added combinational depth (which raises power_index and lowers Fmax)."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands 50 MSPS throughput with max_abs_err <= 2^-12, minimising power_index. Only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me, and both are shared-datapath (low power) families, so the whole budget goes to them. The binding tension is throughput: iterative needs Fmax ~ 50*(N+3) MHz, which is very high for sky130 sc_hd, while unrolled_k relaxes this to 50*(ceil(N/k)+3) MHz. I therefore give iterative the larger share (0.55) because it is the lowest-power family and I want to confirm whether any point on it can meet throughput at all (if not, the front collapses to unrolled_k), and unrolled_k 0.45 to map the k-vs-Fmax trade-off that is most likely to yield feasible, low-power designs. Accuracy ranges (n_iter 12..16, data_width 14..18, both guards, both roundings) are kept identical across families so the merged front reflects a true power-vs-accuracy trade-off rather than an artefact of different search boxes. I deliberately cap n_iter at 16 and data_width at 18: beyond that, accuracy is already well past 2^-12 and only power/area grow, so the hypervolume gain is negligible and the budget is better spent resolving the throughput-feasibility boundary."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The throughput constraint (>= 50 MSPS) is the hard blocker. The iterative and unrolled_k families are fundamentally limited by their serial or partially-parallel execution models; even with aggressive unrolling (k=3) and high Fmax estimates, the best observed throughput was only 9.07 MSPS, which is more than 5x below the 50 MSPS requirement. The pipelined and pipelined_m families are the only architectures in the registry capable of achieving 1 result/cycle throughput, which is necessary to meet the 50 MSPS target at reasonable clock frequencies. Therefore, I must explore the pipelined families to find any feasible designs.",
